 "transcript": {
  "answer": "I can't tell you what the cash rate will be in March 2027. Nobody can, and the RBA doesn't give a fixed path. Here is where things stand in the data.\n\n**Where the cash rate is now**\n- The cash rate target is about 4.35%. The September 2026 monthly average was 4.36%, and it has held at 4.35% since June.\n- It rose from 3.60% in January 2026 to 4.35% by May. That is about 75 basis points of hikes, most likely three 25 bp moves.\n\n**Inflation is still above target**\n- Headline CPI was 4.0% over the year to August 2026. It was 3.5% in July and peaked at 4.6% in March.\n- Trimmed mean, the RBA's preferred measure, was 3.6% in August. That is the highest in this run, and it has risen steadily from 3.2% in September 2025.\n\n**The labour market is softening**\n- Unemployment was 4.72% in September 2026. It was 4.08% in January and 4.50% in April.\n\n**What this means for March 2027**\n- **Most likely:** another hold at 4.35%. Rates have been unchanged for about four months, but trimmed mean inflation is still rising.\n- **Hike:** this becomes more likely if trimmed mean inflation keeps climbing above 3.6%.\n- **Cut:** this becomes more likely if unemployment keeps rising toward 5% and inflation eases.\n- The next quarterly CPI (September quarter) comes out in late October. That release and the unemployment figures will probably shape the RBA's view the most.\n\nI haven't looked at market pricing, such as bond yields or futures. That would show what investors expect, and I can pull the RBA bond yield data if you'd like. This is context, not financial advice.",
